#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684216 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x-11}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3x-11.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{(3x-11)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684298 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac17a5c3ebae52349c2572235271ce3ae66d10a82e703676b42dab77ff5ced64.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "Every arrow points down and left, and the arrows have the same length and direction throughout the diagram.\n\n- The horizontal component is negative. This rules out $H$, whose horizontal component is positive, and $K$, whose horizontal component is zero.\n- The vertical component is negative. This rules out $F$, whose vertical component is positive.\n\nBoth components are constant, so the matching function is\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684300 :question/problem "![](images/66/66f74a13da54fafcdd3989878c69aaf4e6bdb57cb8fe44725b35fbb560b618dc.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "All arrows are horizontal, so we compare their horizontal components at different locations.\n\n- Above the $x$-axis the arrows point right, while below it they point left. This rules out the constant fields $F$ and $H$: $F$ always points right, and $H$ always points left.\n- For positive $y$, the horizontal component is positive. This rules out $G(x,y)=-y\\,\\mathbf{i}$, which points left above the $x$-axis.\n\nAs a further check, arrows in each horizontal row are identical, and their lengths increase with distance from the $x$-axis. Thus the horizontal component changes with $y$, with its sign matching the sign of $y$, and is zero on the $x$-axis. The matching function is\n\n$$\nK(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf{i}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684302 :question/problem "![](images/b0/b056c72881c0a31d7bcd29a1f8edd887edf5b415340558969bbe0b1723d51a69.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "We need to match both components, since the arrows change direction across the plane.\n\n- On the positive $y$-axis, the arrows point right. This rules out $G$, which points left there, and $H$, which points down there. Both $F$ and $K$ remain possible from this observation alone.\n- On the positive $x$-axis, the arrows point down. This rules out $K$, which points up there. This observation alone would leave $F$ and $G$, but $G$ was already ruled out on the positive $y$-axis. Hence $F$ is the only listed function consistent with both axes.\n\nWe can also check the component patterns away from the axes. The horizontal component is positive above the $x$-axis, negative below it, and grows in magnitude with $|y|$; within each row it stays constant. The vertical component is negative to the right of the $y$-axis, positive to its left, and grows in magnitude with $|x|$; within each column it stays constant. At the origin both components are zero.\n\nThese checks agree with\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=y\\,\\mathbf{i}-x\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684976 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d26eff05658cee07957b536af454b14c68287d080d2ce7f70f97aec1abd0f913.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (the $i$ component) is positive and stays constant across the plane. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y)&=-i-2\\,j \\\\ K(x,y)&=-j \\\\ H(x,y)&=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (the $j$ component) is positive and stays constant across the plane.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=i+j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684980 :question/problem "![](images/36/36c9003fd50c9b3bb70dbed870876d71fd93130f16335eac34c20869ea8b14e8.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (the $i$ component) is negative and stays constant across the plane. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(x,y)&=i+j \\\\ K(x,y)&=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (the $j$ component) is negative and stays constant across the plane. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y)=-i-2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684996 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf8da09a26e734ce65f67a499a9ebf0f43ae7560dbdda11ce0339ee224f9c528.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (the $i$ component) varies with the horizontal coordinate $x$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $x=0$, where it is zero. It is positive when $x>0$ and negative when $x<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y)&=-y\\,\\mathbf{i}+x\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ K(x,y)&=-y\\,\\mathbf{i}-y\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (the $j$ component) varies with the horizontal coordinate $x$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $x=0$, where it is zero. It is positive when $x>0$ and negative when $x<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=x\\,\\mathbf{i}-y\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=x\\,\\mathbf{i}+x\\,\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186684999 :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4891b9b900304e5fbd2e70051bb422b9e77b2082374f2ece1ce360fd2010317.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (the $i$ component) varies with the vertical coordinate $y$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $y=0$, where it is zero. It is negative when $y>0$ and positive when $y<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(x,y)&=-x\\,\\mathbf{i}+y\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ K(x,y)&=-x\\,\\mathbf{i}+x\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (the $j$ component) varies with the vertical coordinate $y$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $y=0$, where it is zero. It is positive when $y>0$ and negative when $y<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-y\\,\\mathbf{i}+x\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=-y\\,\\mathbf{i}+y\\,\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685005 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc2d6f58dc09ecb378d91c26b48107b0c31d72d2b4231d1ccc0357ebbdbc6b24.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (the $i$ component) varies with the horizontal coordinate $x$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $x=0$, where it is zero. It is negative when $x>0$ and positive when $x<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y)&=-y\\,\\mathbf{i}+x\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ G(x,y)&=-y\\,\\mathbf{i}+y\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (the $j$ component) varies with the vertical coordinate $y$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $y=0$, where it is zero. It is positive when $y>0$ and negative when $y<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\nK(x,y)=-x\\,\\mathbf{i}+x\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-x\\,\\mathbf{i}+y\\,\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685015 :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c156d9f0830e402074b01d4813b69b70adab5f63a27056a6a3ae6f3bd464f14.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nDots denote zero vectors." :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (the $i$ component) varies with the horizontal coordinate $x$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $x=0$, where it is zero. It is negative when $x>0$ and positive when $x<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y)&=y\\,\\mathbf{i}-x\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ G(x,y)&=y\\,\\mathbf{i}+y\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (the $j$ component) varies with the horizontal coordinate $x$. Its magnitude grows as we move away from $x=0$, where it is zero. It is negative when $x>0$ and positive when $x<0$. Therefore, we can rule out:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-x\\,\\mathbf{i}+y\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=-x\\,\\mathbf{i}-x\\,\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685032 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could generate the vector field above? Check each proposed field against the arrows throughout the diagram.\n\nArrow lengths are drawn using a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $y$-axis, point opposite its positive direction, and keep the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must have zero $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf k$ components and a constant negative $\\mathbf j$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf F=-\\mathbf k$ points along the $z$-axis, so it has the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf G=-x^2\\,\\mathbf j$ has the right axis and points in the negative $y$-direction when $x\\ne0$. However, its length is $x^2$, so it varies as $x$ changes and is zero at $x=0$. The diagram instead shows nonzero arrows of equal length, including on the plane $x=0$.\n- $\\mathbf H=\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero $\\mathbf i$ component, so its arrows are not parallel to the $y$-axis.\n- $\\mathbf K=\\mathbf j$ points in the positive $y$-direction, opposite the arrows.\n\nEvery formula is ruled out, so the correct response is **None of the listed**. A constant field such as $-\\mathbf j$ would have the observed pattern, but it is not among the listed formulas."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685035 :question/problem "![](images/bf/bff60466ca8abba4fd849c046c02af3a4811279774975fc981cafad91d734ed5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\nArrow lengths are drawn using a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $x$-axis, so their $\\mathbf j$ and $\\mathbf k$ components are zero. This rules out $\\mathbf H=-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j$, which has a nonzero $\\mathbf j$ component.\n\nThe positive $x$-axis points toward the lower left; the arrows point the other way. Their $\\mathbf i$ component is therefore negative, ruling out $\\mathbf G=\\mathbf i$.\n\nThe arrows have the same length and direction throughout the diagram, including across different $y$-positions. The field $\\mathbf F=-y\\,\\mathbf i$ would instead change its magnitude with $y$, vanish at $y=0$, and reverse direction across that plane. It does not fit.\n\nThus $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf i$ could generate the diagram: it is constant and points in the negative $x$-direction. “None of the listed” is incorrect."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685037 :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a0a919af689e9c332ba6ceb3d606208dab9eabadef9b4871ac8e164843ebe88.png)\n\nCompare the arrows with the positive directions of the labeled axes. The listed formulas are constant vector fields. Which could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths are drawn using a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "Each arrow is parallel to the $z$-axis and points in its positive direction. Thus the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components are zero, while the $\\mathbf k$ component is positive.\n\nThe field $\\mathbf F=-\\mathbf k$ points in the opposite direction. The fields $\\mathbf G=\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf K=\\mathbf j$ point along the wrong axes.\n\nAll the displayed arrows have the same length, consistent with a constant field. Therefore $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=\\mathbf k$ could generate the diagram. Since a listed formula fits, “None of the listed” is incorrect."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685343 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5a5575a4fbdbd2b997ef94b9b1de64b00139b51efd8023fb88dbde0550df921.png)\n\nCheck each proposed field against the arrows throughout the diagram. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $y$-axis and point in its **positive** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant positive $\\mathbf j$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=e^{y}\\,\\mathbf j$ varies in magnitude as $y$ changes; the displayed arrows instead have equal lengths.\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\mathbf j$ is constant and has the observed axis and direction, so it fits.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=- y\\,\\mathbf j$ varies as $y$ changes and is zero on $y=0$; the diagram has nonzero arrows of equal length there as well.\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=y^{2}\\,\\mathbf j$ varies as $y$ changes and is zero on $y=0$; the diagram has nonzero arrows of equal length there as well.\n\nTherefore $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\mathbf j$ could generate the field."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685346 :question/problem "![](images/69/690db47f3c87c0374217af2eba3acb05f73181224f2694a71a46bc1f0d5ee417.png)\n\nCheck each proposed field against the arrows throughout the diagram. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $z$-axis and point in its **negative** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant negative $\\mathbf k$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=- y\\,\\mathbf k$ varies as $y$ changes and is zero on $y=0$; the diagram has nonzero arrows of equal length there as well.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=\\mathbf k$ points in the opposite direction.\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=e^{y}\\,\\mathbf k$ varies in magnitude as $y$ changes; the displayed arrows instead have equal lengths.\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=- y^{2}\\,\\mathbf k$ varies as $y$ changes and is zero on $y=0$; the diagram has nonzero arrows of equal length there as well.\n\nTherefore every listed formula is ruled out. The correct response is **None of the listed**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685397 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fec3ce0d1886d6ee9a568aad2fab68039e282b2bd93c69196628731c791854e.png)\n\nCompare the arrows with the positive directions of the labeled axes. All listed formulas are constant vector fields. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $x$-axis and point in its **positive** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant positive $\\mathbf i$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf i$ points in the opposite direction.\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n\nTherefore every listed formula is ruled out. The correct response is **None of the listed**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685398 :question/problem "![](images/69/690db47f3c87c0374217af2eba3acb05f73181224f2694a71a46bc1f0d5ee417.png)\n\nCompare the arrows with the positive directions of the labeled axes. All listed formulas are constant vector fields. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $z$-axis and point in its **negative** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant negative $\\mathbf k$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ is constant and has the observed axis and direction, so it fits.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf i$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=\\mathbf k$ points in the opposite direction.\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n\nTherefore $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ could generate the field."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685402 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f5e69364a876f0714abedacd9e8cecd282826318e12408e8943b1e824dcde09.png)\n\nCompare the arrows with the positive directions of the labeled axes. All listed formulas are constant vector fields. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $x$-axis and point in its **negative** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant negative $\\mathbf i$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=\\mathbf k$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n\nTherefore every listed formula is ruled out. The correct response is **None of the listed**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186685403 :question/problem "![](images/c3/c324583aba5224e8c2ba9b2dc75c8836fd0e267c80a3f4119f7c67536d92bfe4.png)\n\nCompare the arrows with the positive directions of the labeled axes. All listed formulas are constant vector fields. Which of the following could generate the field shown?\n\nArrow lengths use a common display scale; no numerical magnitude is specified." :question/worked-solution "The arrows are parallel to the $y$-axis and point in its **negative** direction. They have the same nonzero length throughout the diagram. A matching field must therefore have zero components on the other axes and a constant negative $\\mathbf j$ component.\n\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y,z)=\\mathbf i$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf k$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf K(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf i$ has a nonzero component on another axis, so its arrows have the wrong direction.\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ is constant and has the observed axis and direction, so it fits.\n\nTherefore $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf j$ could generate the field."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687392 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sin(x-2y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f$ is a potential function for a vector field $\\mathbf F$ when $\\mathbf F=\\nabla f$. To identify the field, differentiate $f$ with respect to each coordinate, holding the other coordinates constant.\n\nThe chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=\\cos(x-2y)\\cdot 1=\\cos(x-2y),\\\\\nf_y&=\\cos(x-2y)\\cdot(-2)=-2\\cos(x-2y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $-2$ in $f_y$ comes from differentiating $x-2y$ with respect to $y$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=f_x\\,\\mathbf i+f_y\\,\\mathbf j=\\cos(x-2y)\\,\\mathbf i-2\\cos(x-2y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687393 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=xy e^{xz}+z^2$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f$ is a potential function for a vector field $\\mathbf F$ when $\\mathbf F=\\nabla f$. To identify the field, differentiate $f$ with respect to each coordinate, holding the other coordinates constant.\n\nWhen differentiating with respect to $x$, both $xy$ and $e^{xz}$ depend on $x$, so we need the product rule as well as the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=y e^{xz}+xy\\bigl(z e^{xz}\\bigr)\\\\\n   &=y(1+xz)e^{xz}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $f_y$, the exponential is constant with respect to $y$. For $f_z$, the factor $xy$ is constant, while both $e^{xz}$ and $z^2$ must be differentiated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=x e^{xz},\\\\\nf_z&=xy\\bigl(x e^{xz}\\bigr)+2z=x^2y e^{xz}+2z.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the components in $x,y,z$ order gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F=\\nabla f=y(1+xz)e^{xz}\\,\\mathbf i+x e^{xz}\\,\\mathbf j+(x^2y e^{xz}+2z)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nA candidate must agree in all three components. Omitting either term from the product rule, retaining an extra $y$ in $f_y$, or missing a term or chain-rule factor in $f_z$ changes the field."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687408 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=\\sin(xy+z)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f$ is a potential function for a vector field $\\mathbf F$ when $\\mathbf F=\\nabla f$. To identify the field, differentiate $f$ with respect to each coordinate, holding the other coordinates constant.\n\nThere are three components. The inner function $xy+z$ has partial derivatives $y$, $x$, and $1$, respectively. Applying the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=y\\cos(xy+z),\\\\\nf_y&=x\\cos(xy+z),\\\\\nf_z&=\\cos(xy+z).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, differentiating with respect to $z$ contributes a factor of $1$, not $z$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F=\\nabla f&=y\\cos(xy+z)\\,\\mathbf i+x\\cos(xy+z)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\&\\quad+\\cos(xy+z)\\,\\mathbf k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687810 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=- 3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 4 z^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,k \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 4 z^{2}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 4 z^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}\\left(- 3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 4 z^{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\ &=-6 x\\,i+8 y\\,j-8 z\\,k \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=-6 x\\,i+8 y\\,j-8 z\\,k.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687811 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=- 4 x^{2} y z - 2 z^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f &=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,i+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,j+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,k \\\\ &=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 4 x^{2} y z - 2 z^{2}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 4 x^{2} y z - 2 z^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad +\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}\\left(- 4 x^{2} y z - 2 z^{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\ &=-8 x y z\\,i-4 x^{2} z\\,j-\\left(4 z+4 x^{2} y\\right)\\,k \\\\ &=F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=-8 x y z\\,i-4 x^{2} z\\,j-\\left(4 z+4 x^{2} y\\right)\\,k.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687828 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y e^{- x z} + 2 z^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A potential function satisfies $F=\\nabla f$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others constant.\n\nFor $f_x$, both factors of $- 2 x y e^{- x z}$ depend on $x$, so the product and chain rules give\n\n$$\nf_x=- 2 ye^{- x z}+(- 2 x y)(- z)e^{- x z}.\n$$\n\nFor $f_y$, the exponential is constant. For $f_z$, apply the chain rule to the exponential and differentiate the added quadratic. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x&=2 x y z e^{- x z} - 2 y e^{- x z} \\\\ f_y&=- 2 x e^{- x z} \\\\ f_z&=2 x^{2} y e^{- x z} + 4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the components in $x,y,z$ order,\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(-2 y e^{- x z}+2 x y z e^{- x z}\\right)\\,i-2 x e^{- x z}\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(4 z+2 x^{2} y e^{- x z}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687833 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=x y e^{2 x z} - 2 z^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A potential function satisfies $F=\\nabla f$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others constant.\n\nFor $f_x$, both factors of $x y e^{2 x z}$ depend on $x$, so the product and chain rules give\n\n$$\nf_x=ye^{2 x z}+(x y)(2 z)e^{2 x z}.\n$$\n\nFor $f_y$, the exponential is constant. For $f_z$, apply the chain rule to the exponential and differentiate the added quadratic. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x&=2 x y z e^{2 x z} + y e^{2 x z} \\\\ f_y&=x e^{2 x z} \\\\ f_z&=2 x^{2} y e^{2 x z} - 4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the components in $x,y,z$ order,\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(1 y e^{2 x z}+2 x y z e^{2 x z}\\right)\\,i+1 x e^{2 x z}\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(-4 z+2 x^{2} y e^{2 x z}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687834 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=2 x y e^{2 x z} - 2 z^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A potential function satisfies $F=\\nabla f$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others constant.\n\nFor $f_x$, both factors of $2 x y e^{2 x z}$ depend on $x$, so the product and chain rules give\n\n$$\nf_x=2 ye^{2 x z}+(2 x y)(2 z)e^{2 x z}.\n$$\n\nFor $f_y$, the exponential is constant. For $f_z$, apply the chain rule to the exponential and differentiate the added quadratic. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x&=4 x y z e^{2 x z} + 2 y e^{2 x z} \\\\ f_y&=2 x e^{2 x z} \\\\ f_z&=4 x^{2} y e^{2 x z} - 4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the components in $x,y,z$ order,\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(2 y e^{2 x z}+4 x y z e^{2 x z}\\right)\\,i+2 x e^{2 x z}\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(-4 z+4 x^{2} y e^{2 x z}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186687836 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y e^{2 y z} - 2 z^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A potential function satisfies $F=\\nabla f$. Differentiate with respect to each coordinate, holding the others constant.\n\nFor $f_y$, both factors of $- 2 x y e^{2 y z}$ depend on $y$, so the product and chain rules give\n\n$$\nf_y=- 2 xe^{2 y z}+(- 2 x y)(2 z)e^{2 y z}.\n$$\n\nFor $f_x$, the exponential is constant. For $f_z$, apply the chain rule to the exponential and differentiate the added quadratic. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x&=- 2 y e^{2 y z} \\\\ f_y&=- 4 x y z e^{2 y z} - 2 x e^{2 y z} \\\\ f_z&=- 4 x y^{2} e^{2 y z} - 4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the components in $x,y,z$ order,\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{aligned}&-2 y e^{2 y z}\\,i-\\left(2 x e^{2 y z}+4 x y z e^{2 y z}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &-\\left(4 z+4 x y^{2} e^{2 y z}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693421 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t,z)=\\langle \\text{_____},\\,3\\tan t,\\,z\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $z\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}-\\frac{y^2}{9}=1,\\qquad x\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$ and $\\sec t\\ge 1$ on this interval. What is the first component of $\\mathbf f$?" :question/worked-solution "The free coordinate $z$ controls movement parallel to the cylinder's axis. In the $xy$-plane, substituting $y=3\\tan t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}=1+\\tan^2 t=\\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nSince $x\\ge 2$ and $\\sec t\\ge 1$ on the given interval, choose the positive root: $x=2\\sec t$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t,z)=\\langle 2\\sec t,\\,3\\tan t,\\,z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstitution verifies the equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(2\\sec t)^2}{4}-\\frac{(3\\tan t)^2}{9}\n=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1.\n$$\n\nAs $t$ runs through $(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$, $3\\tan t$ takes every real value and $2\\sec t$ gives the corresponding point on the positive branch. Letting $z$ range over all real numbers gives the whole sheet. The first component is $2\\sec t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693424 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(x,t)=\\langle x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{2}\\tan t\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n2y^2-3z^2=6,\\qquad y\\ge\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the second component of $\\mathbf f$?" :question/worked-solution "Divide the Cartesian equation by $6$ to identify the two squared scale factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{3}-\\frac{z^2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nThe cross-section lies in the $yz$-plane and $x$ is free. With $z=\\sqrt{2}\\tan t$, the normalized equation gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{3}=1+\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}\\tan t)^2}{2}\n=1+\\tan^2 t=\\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nThe scale for $y$ is $\\sqrt{3}$, not $3$. Since $\\sec t\\ge 1$ on the given interval and $y\\ge\\sqrt{3}$, the correct sign is positive:\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{3}\\sec t.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(x,t)=\\langle x,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\sec t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\tan t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck directly in the original equation:\n\n$$\n2(\\sqrt{3}\\sec t)^2-3(\\sqrt{2}\\tan t)^2\n=6(\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t)=6.\n$$\n\nThe tangent coordinate takes every real $z$, the secant coordinate supplies the corresponding positive $y$, and arbitrary real $x$ extends this branch along the cylinder's axis. The second component is $\\sqrt{3}\\sec t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693426 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t,y)=\\langle \\text{_____},\\,y,\\,3\\sec t\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{9}-\\frac{x^2}{4}=1,\\qquad z\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $\\mathbf f$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation involves $x$ and $z$, so $y$ is the free coordinate. Substituting $z=3\\sec t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}=\\frac{(3\\sec t)^2}{9}-1\n=\\sec^2 t-1=\\tan^2 t.\n$$\n\nThe choice $x=2\\tan t$ satisfies this relation for every $t$ in the given interval. Thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t,y)=\\langle 2\\tan t,\\,y,\\,3\\sec t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nChecking the base curve,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(3\\sec t)^2}{9}-\\frac{(2\\tan t)^2}{4}\n=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\sec t\\ge 1$ on $(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$, the sheet condition $z\\ge 3$ holds. The tangent coordinate covers all real $x$, while arbitrary real $y$ extends the base curve along the cylinder's axis. The first component is $2\\tan t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693601 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=\\langle \\text{_____},\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\sec t\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n5z^2-3x^2=15,\\qquad z\\ge \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $f$?" :question/worked-solution "Divide the Cartesian equation by $15$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{3}-\\frac{x^2}{5}=1\n$$\n\nThe base curve uses $z$ and $x$; $y$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{5}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{3}\\sec t)^2}{3}-1=\\sec^2 t-1=\\tan^2 t.\n$$\n\nChoose $x=\\sqrt{5}\\tan t$, which takes every real value on the given interval. This is the positive-tangent orientation used in the choices. The scale is $\\sqrt{5}=\\sqrt{5}$. Thus\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\tan t,\\qquad f(t,y)=\\langle \\sqrt{5}\\tan t,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\sec t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3}\\sec t)^2}{3}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5}\\tan t)^2}{5}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $y$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the first component is $\\sqrt{5}\\tan t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693602 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=\\langle \\text{_____},\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{7}\\tan t\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n7x^2-3z^2=21,\\qquad x\\ge \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the first component of $f$?" :question/worked-solution "Divide the Cartesian equation by $21$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{3}-\\frac{z^2}{7}=1\n$$\n\nThe base curve uses $x$ and $z$; $y$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{3}=1+\\frac{(\\sqrt{7}\\tan t)^2}{7}=1+\\tan^2 t=\\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nSince $x\\ge \\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sec t\\ge1$ on the given interval, take the positive root. The scale is $\\sqrt{3}=\\sqrt{3}$. Thus\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\sec t,\\qquad f(t,y)=\\langle \\sqrt{3}\\sec t,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{7}\\tan t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\sqrt{3}\\sec t)^2}{3}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{7}\\tan t)^2}{7}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $y$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the first component is $\\sqrt{3}\\sec t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693612 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\nf(t,x)=\\langle x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2\\sec t\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{4}-\\frac{y^2}{1}=1,\\qquad z\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$. What is the second component of $f$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already normalized. The base curve uses $z$ and $y$; $x$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{1}=\\frac{(2\\sec t)^2}{4}-1=\\sec^2 t-1=\\tan^2 t.\n$$\n\nChoose $y=\\tan t$, which takes every real value on the given interval. This is the positive-tangent orientation used in the choices. The scale is $1=\\sqrt{1}$. Thus\n\n$$\ny=\\tan t,\\qquad f(t,x)=\\langle x,\\,\\tan t,\\,2\\sec t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2\\sec t)^2}{4}-\\frac{(\\tan t)^2}{1}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $x$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the second component is $\\tan t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693621 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=\\langle 3\\sec t,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}-\\frac{z^2}{16}=1,\\qquad x\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$. What is the third component of $f$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already normalized. The base curve uses $x$ and $z$; $y$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{16}=\\frac{(3\\sec t)^2}{9}-1=\\sec^2 t-1=\\tan^2 t.\n$$\n\nChoose $z=4\\tan t$, which takes every real value on the given interval. This is the positive-tangent orientation used in the choices. The scale is $4=\\sqrt{16}$. Thus\n\n$$\nz=4\\tan t,\\qquad f(t,y)=\\langle 3\\sec t,\\,y,\\,4\\tan t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3\\sec t)^2}{9}-\\frac{(4\\tan t)^2}{16}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $y$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the third component is $4\\tan t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693630 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=\\langle \\tan t,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{16}-\\frac{x^2}{1}=1,\\qquad z\\ge 4.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$. What is the third component of $f$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already normalized. The base curve uses $z$ and $x$; $y$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{z^2}{16}=1+\\frac{(\\tan t)^2}{1}=1+\\tan^2 t=\\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ge 4$ and $\\sec t\\ge1$ on the given interval, take the positive root. The scale is $4=\\sqrt{16}$. Thus\n\n$$\nz=4\\sec t,\\qquad f(t,y)=\\langle \\tan t,\\,y,\\,4\\sec t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(4\\sec t)^2}{16}-\\frac{(\\tan t)^2}{1}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $y$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the third component is $4\\sec t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693633 :question/problem "The function\n\n$$\nf(t,z)=\\langle 4\\tan t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z\\rangle\n$$\n\nwith $-\\pi/2<t<\\pi/2$ and $z\\in\\mathbb R$ parametrizes the sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{1}-\\frac{x^2}{16}=1,\\qquad y\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nUse $\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t=1$. What is the second component of $f$?" :question/worked-solution "The equation is already normalized. The base curve uses $y$ and $x$; $z$ is the free coordinate. Substituting the supplied component gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{1}=1+\\frac{(4\\tan t)^2}{16}=1+\\tan^2 t=\\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 1$ and $\\sec t\\ge1$ on the given interval, take the positive root. The scale is $1=\\sqrt{1}$. Thus\n\n$$\ny=\\sec t,\\qquad f(t,z)=\\langle 4\\tan t,\\,\\sec t,\\,z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCheck in the normalized equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\sec t)^2}{1}-\\frac{(4\\tan t)^2}{16}&=\\sec^2 t-\\tan^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe secant coordinate satisfies the positive-sheet condition. The tangent coordinate ranges over all real values, and arbitrary real $z$ extends the base curve along the cylinder axis. Therefore the second component is $\\sec t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693797 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x\\sin(2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose $u=x$ and $v'=\\sin(2x)$, so $u'=1$ and $v=-\\frac12\\cos(2x)$.\n\nSubstitute into the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI\n&=\\left[-\\frac{x\\cos(2x)}2\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}\n+\\frac12\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos(2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\left[-\\frac{x\\cos(2x)}2+\\frac{\\sin(2x)}4\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute the upper limit and subtract the value at the lower limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI\n&=\\left(-\\frac{\\pi\\cos\\pi}{4}+\\frac{\\sin\\pi}{4}\\right)\n-\\left(0+\\frac{\\sin0}{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac\\pi4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere the remaining integral contributes zero; the nonzero boundary term determines the result."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693799 :question/problem "Apply integration by parts once to\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 x e^{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct rewrite with $u=x$ and $v'=e^{2x}$. Leave the remaining integral unevaluated." :question/worked-solution "With $u=x$ and $v'=e^{2x}$, we have $u'=1$ and $v=\\frac12 e^{2x}$. The factor $\\frac12$ is needed because the exponent has derivative $2$.\n\nUse the definite integration-by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b uv'\\,\\mathrm{d}x=[uv]_a^b-\\int_a^b vu'\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^1 x e^{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\left[\\frac{x e^{2x}}2\\right]_0^1-\\int_0^1\\frac{e^{2x}}2\\cdot1\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{x e^{2x}}2\\right]_0^1-\\frac12\\int_0^1e^{2x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe boundary term and the remaining integral both retain the original limits. This is the requested rewrite."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186693808 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral. Give an exact answer.\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2} x\\cos(2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the given integral. Choose $u=x$ and $v'=\\cos(2x)$. Then $u'=1$ and $v=\\frac12\\sin(2x)$.\n\nIntegration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI\n&=\\left[\\frac{x\\sin(2x)}2\\right]_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}\n-\\frac12\\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}\\sin(2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{x\\sin(2x)}2+\\frac{\\cos(2x)}4\\right]_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $F(x)=\\frac{x\\sin(2x)}2+\\frac{\\cos(2x)}4$, the two endpoint values are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(\\pi/2)&=\\frac{\\pi\\sin\\pi}{4}+\\frac{\\cos\\pi}{4}=-\\frac14,\\\\\nF(\\pi/4)&=\\frac{\\pi\\sin(\\pi/2)}8+\\frac{\\cos(\\pi/2)}4=\\frac\\pi8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire lower-endpoint value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=F(\\pi/2)-F(\\pi/4)\\\\&=-\\frac14-\\frac\\pi8\\\\&=-\\frac{\\pi+2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth endpoints contribute. The negative result also agrees with $x>0$ and $\\cos(2x)<0$ inside the interval."}]}
